Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• USERS."age":  determine the youngest and oldest age values.  
• USERS."gender": segregate results by gender (e.g., 'F', 'M', ‘O’ …).  
• USERS."created_at": apply the 2019‑01‑01 → 2022‑04‑30 window by filtering its micro‑seconds‑since‑epoch numeric range.  
• USERS."id": make user‐level counts unique.  

Possible conditions to narrow the time window (numeric micro‑seconds since 1970‑01‑01 UTC)  
• 2019‑01‑01 00:00:00  =  1546300800000000  
• 2022‑04‑30 23:59:59 ≈ 1651363199000000  
Thus you can use  
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that move from simple inspection toward the final aggregation.  
All identifiers are enclosed in double quotation marks as required.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at a handful of raw rows in USERS to understand values in the key columns.
SELECT "id", "gender", "age", "created_at"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Distinct gender values present in the USERS table.
SELECT DISTINCT "gender"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Minimum and maximum age overall to learn the global boundaries.
SELECT MIN("age")  AS "min_age",
       MAX("age")  AS "max_age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Minimum and maximum age by gender (no date filter yet).
SELECT "gender",
       MIN("age") AS "youngest_age",
       MAX("age") AS "oldest_age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
GROUP BY "gender"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Identify the youngest‑age users (global) and list a sample of them.
WITH youngest AS (
    SELECT MIN("age") AS "youngest_age" FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
)
SELECT u."id", u."gender", u."age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u, youngest y
WHERE u."age" = y."youngest_age"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Identify the oldest‑age users (global) and list a sample of them.
WITH oldest AS (
    SELECT MAX("age") AS "oldest_age" FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
)
SELECT u."id", u."gender", u."age"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u, oldest o
WHERE u."age" = o."oldest_age"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Youngest and oldest age values per gender restricted to 2019‑01‑01 → 2022‑04‑30.
SELECT "gender",
       MIN("age") AS "youngest_age_2019_2022",
       MAX("age") AS "oldest_age_2019_2022"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP BY "gender"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Count of users at the youngest age for each gender inside the time window.
WITH age_bounds AS (
    SELECT "gender",
           MIN("age") AS "youngest_age"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY "gender"
)
SELECT u."gender",
       COUNT(*) AS "cnt_youngest_users"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u
JOIN age_bounds b
  ON u."gender" = b."gender"
 AND u."age"    = b."youngest_age"
WHERE u."created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP BY u."gender"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Count of users at the oldest age for each gender inside the time window.
WITH age_bounds AS (
    SELECT "gender",
           MAX("age") AS "oldest_age"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY "gender"
)
SELECT u."gender",
       COUNT(*) AS "cnt_oldest_users"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u
JOIN age_bounds b
  ON u."gender" = b."gender"
 AND u."age"    = b."oldest_age"
WHERE u."created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP BY u."gender"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Combine youngest and oldest user counts for each gender in one result set (final aggregation idea).
WITH bounds AS (
    SELECT "gender",
           MIN("age") AS "min_age",
           MAX("age") AS "max_age"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS"
    WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY "gender"
), youngest AS (
    SELECT u."gender", COUNT(*) AS "youngest_cnt"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u
    JOIN bounds b
      ON u."gender" = b."gender"
     AND u."age"    = b."min_age"
    WHERE u."created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY u."gender"
), oldest AS (
    SELECT u."gender", COUNT(*) AS "oldest_cnt"
    FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."USERS" u
    JOIN bounds b
      ON u."gender" = b."gender"
     AND u."age"    = b."max_age"
    WHERE u."created_at" BETWEEN 1546300800000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY u."gender"
)
SELECT COALESCE(y."gender", o."gender") AS "gender",
       y."youngest_cnt",
       o."oldest_cnt"
FROM youngest y
FULL OUTER JOIN oldest o
  ON y."gender" = o."gender"
LIMIT 20;
```
